{
 "id": "2026-10-31-photonic-chip-links-telescopes",
 "lang": "en",
 "script": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "A glass chip just made two telescopes act as one. What can it see?"
  },
  {
   "id": "f1",
   "text": "Researchers at Heriot-Watt University in Scotland built the chip, with colleagues in Germany and the United States, the university says. It is a couple of centimetres across. Ultrafast lasers write tiny channels for light inside the glass."
  },
  {
   "id": "f2",
   "text": "The chip sits inside an instrument called CHARIOT at the CHARA Array in California, which links six telescopes. Observing campaigns in 2024 and 2025 combined light from two of them and recorded signals from a range of stars, according to the team."
  },
  {
   "id": "f3",
   "text": "Joining telescopes this way gives sharper detail than either could alone. Coverage of the study, published in Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society, says the chip detects fainter stars than the previous system. It has two inputs now, and could be upgraded to four."
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Will chips like this link many more telescopes? Yes or no?"
  }
 ],
